Week 01 – How Computers and the Internet Work
This document contains beginner-friendly notes for Week 01. 
Students will learn the basic concepts of computers, the internet, browsers, websites, DNS, networks, files, folders, and cloud storage.
Computer Hardware and Software
Hardware means the physical parts of a computer that we can see and touch, such as a keyboard, mouse, monitor, CPU, RAM, and printer. Software means programs and applications like Windows, Chrome, Microsoft Word, and VS Code.
Operating Systems
An operating system is the main software that manages the computer. Examples include Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, and iOS.
How the Internet Works
The internet is a global network of connected devices. When you open a website, your device sends a request to a server, and the server sends information back.
Browsers and Websites
A browser is software used to open websites, such as Google Chrome, Edge, or Firefox. A website is a collection of web pages.
DNS and Domain Names
DNS stands for Domain Name System. It helps convert easy website names like google.com into IP addresses.
Search Engines
Search engines like Google and Bing help users find information on the internet.
Understanding Networks
A network is a group of connected devices that can share information.
How Data Travels Across the Internet
Data travels in small packets through networks and reaches its destination.
Files, Folders, and Cloud Storage
Files store information, folders organize files, and cloud storage services like Google Drive keep files online.
Hands-on Activity
Create an organized folder structure on your computer and upload important files to Google Drive.
